==Specifications==
The Gemera is the company's first four-seater car.  

=== Powertrain ===
The hybrid powertrain uses an updated hot-V variant of the 5.0-litre twin-turbo HV8 from the [[Koenigsegg Jesko|Jesko]]. The HV8 powertrain has a combined power output of {{convert|1715|kW|kW hp PS|abbr=on|order=out}} and {{convert|2750|Nm|lbft|0|abbr=on}} of torque<ref>{{cite web |last=Miller |first=Caleb |date=11 July 2023 |title=Koenigsegg Gemera Boosts Output to 2269 HP with New Optional V-8 |url=https://www.caranddriver.com/news/a44506609/koenigsegg-gemera-production-v8-details/ |accessdate=11 July 2023}}</ref> when the engine runs on E85 fuel. The HV8 engine is mated to a 9-speed Light Speed Tourbillon Transmission (LSTT), a modified version of the 9-speed Light Speed Transmission found in the Jesko. 

Initially, Koenigsegg planned to offer an option of a hybrid powertrain using their twin-turbocharged [[Koenigsegg TFG|TFG]] 2.0-litre inline-3, which would have been mated to a direct drive (single-speed transmission). However, in July, 2024, Christian Von Koenigsegg said that the inline-3 powertrain option would not be offered in the Gemera due to a lack of demand from customers.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Alaniz |first=Anthony |date=22 July 2024 |title=Koenigsegg Won’t Build the Three-Cylinder Gemera Because No One Wants It |url=https://www.motor1.com/news/727458/koenigsegg-gemera-three-cylinder-engine/ |website=Motor1}}</ref> Notably, this engine features Koenigsegg's Freevalve technology, which means it doesn't use a [[camshaft]]. The engine produced {{Convert|600|bhp|kW PS|0|abbr=on}} at 7,500 rpm and {{Convert|600|Nm|lbft|0|abbr=on}} from 2,000-7,000 rpm, with a redline of 8,500 rpm. Combined with the three electric motors, this powertrain produced a combined {{Convert|1400|hp|kW PS|0|abbr=on}}. 

==== Electric motors ====
Three electric motors are included, one for each rear wheel with {{convert|500|hp|kW PS hp|0|abbr=on|order=out}} and 1000 Nm each and the Dark Matter motor on the front axle with 600 kW (800 hp) and 1250 Nm.<ref name="Dark matter">{{Cite web |last=Salas |first=Joe |date=2025-04-26 |title=Dark Matter: An 86-lb, 800-hp EV motor by Koenigsegg |url=https://newatlas.com/automotive/dark-matter-koenigsegg-ev-motor/ |access-date=2025-06-11 |website=New Atlas |language=en-US}}</ref>

The Dark Matter motor is {{cvt|15.1|in|order=flip}} in diameter and {{cvt|15.3|in|order=flip}} thick, for a total weight of {{Convert|39|kg}}. It uses a "raxial flux" design; an [[Axial flux motor|axial flux]] design complemented by a smaller [[Radial flux motor|radial flux]] component to flatten the torque response curve and reduce torque ripple. It uses [[carbon fiber]] to minimize weight for the rotor, stator, and housing. It is a six-phase motor instead of the conventional three-phase design.<ref name="Dark matter" />

It comes with an 850V 14 kWh battery pack.<ref name="Dark matter" />

=== Wheels, brakes and tyres ===
The Gemera features standard forged aluminium centre lock wheels, with a size of 21" x 10.5" up front and 22" x 11.5" at the back. However, optional Aircore carbon fibre wheels are available. 

The standard tyres are Michelin Pilot Sport 4S, while the car can be had with Michelin Cup 2R (still street legal), with both coming in a 295/30 ZR21 size for the front axle and in a 315/30 ZR22 size for the rear axle.

The front brakes use {{convert|410|mm|in|1|abbr=in}} ventilated ceramic rotors with 6-piston calipers, while the rear brakes feature {{convert|395|mm|in|1|abbr=in}} ventilated ceramic discs with 4-piston calipers, while the car features electric brake boosting.<ref name=":0">{{cite web |title=Gemera - Technical Specifications |url=https://www.koenigsegg.com/technical-specifications-gemera |accessdate=28 Dec 2021}}</ref>

=== Other features ===
The Gemera is Koenigsegg's first all-wheel-drive model and has all-wheel steering and [[torque vectoring]]. In line with other Koenigsegg models, the chassis has a carbon fibre monocoque with aluminium sub-structures. The car features electronically adjustable ride height and a titanium exhaust system manufactured by [[Akrapovič]].

Two notable design features of the Gemera are its lack of a [[B-pillar]] and two large [[dihedral synchro-helix actuation doors]], which open forward to allow easier access to the leather upholstered four-seater cabin. Interior features include eight cup-holders (four heated and four cooled), driver assistance systems, four touchscreens (two 13-inch central touchscreens along with two additional screens for the side and rear view cameras), infotainment system for the front and rear passengers, front and rear wireless phone chargers, Apple CarPlay, Wi-Fi connection, a three-zone climate system, electrically adjustable seats with [[memory foam]] padding, and an audio system with 11 speakers. Koenigsegg announced that production will be limited to 300 
cars.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.caranddriver.com/news/a31206198/koenigsegg-gemera-photos-info/|title=1677-HP Koenigsegg Gemera Has 3 Electric Motors, 4 Seats, 8 Cupholders|date=3 March 2020|first=Connor|last=Hoffman|publisher=[[Car & Driver]]|accessdate=6 March 2020}}</ref><ref name="Motor1">{{cite web|url=https://www.motor1.com/news/402027/koenigsegg-gemera-4-seat-unveiled/|title=Koenigsegg Gemera Debuts As Brand's First 4-Seat, 3-Cylinder Model|date=3 March 2020|first=Anthony|last=Karr|publisher=Motor1|accessdate=6 March 2020}}</ref>

In addition, the presentation introduced the optional Ghost package for the Gemera, consisting of an extended front splitter, S-duct, rear wing and other aerodynamic features.

In 2022, Koenigsegg announced plans to expand its operations with the construction of a new factory in which the Gemera will be built. The new addition will contain a production & assembly line, a customer lounge, offices, spaces for events, as well as additional warehousing and pre-production spaces.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Koenigsegg takes its expansion to the next level |url=https://news.cision.com/koenigsegg/r/koenigsegg-takes-its-expansion-to-the-next-level,c3499229 |access-date=2022-09-11 |website=News Powered by Cision |language=en}}</ref> When complete, the Koenigsegg factory will total over {{cvt|30,000|sqm|sqft}}.

=== Performance ===
The Gemera's I3 version has a claimed top speed of {{convert|400|kph|0|abbr=on}}, accelerating from 0 to {{convert|100|kph|abbr=on}} in 1.9 seconds, with a top speed of {{convert|300|kph|0|abbr=on}} in electric mode.<ref name=":2" /><ref name=":3" /><ref name="Motor1"/>

